            <ArticleTitle>Self-mutilation in a child with a tethered spinal cord. Case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>228-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Self-mutilation after deafferentation injuries has rarely been reported in humans. The authors report on a 16-year-old girl who was born with a myelomeningocele. In adolescence it was noted that concurrent with her spinal cord becoming symptomatically tethered she began to self-mutilate her digits. A rare manifestation of the tethered spinal cord may be dysesthesias that led to self-mutilation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
